      <h3 style="margin-top:32px;">What Shopify Flow actually is</h3>
      <p>Flow triggers on Shopify-internal events — order created, order paid, order cancelled, customer created, product added, inventory level changed — and performs Shopify-internal actions: add tags, cancel orders, send internal notifications, update metafields. Within that boundary it is genuinely useful and genuinely free.</p>
      <p>What it cannot do without paid third-party connectors is leave Shopify. It cannot write to Google Sheets, call the Meta Conversions API, post to Slack, or hit any external HTTP endpoint. <strong>Every automation on this page involves sending data outside Shopify, so none of them are buildable in Flow.</strong> It is also gated to the Shopify, Advanced and Plus plans — a Basic store does not have it at all.</p>
      <p>The practical split: use Flow for internal store logic — auto-tagging high-value customers, hiding out-of-stock products, internal low-stock notifications. Use Make.com for anything that crosses the boundary. They are complements, not competitors, and a store on Advanced or Plus can reasonably run both.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the webhook difference decides it against Zapier</h3>
      <p>Every automation described here starts with a Shopify Order Payment webhook. <strong>Zapier's free plan does not support webhook triggers</strong>, which means this stack cannot be built on free Zapier in any combination of Zaps — the blocker is structural, not a question of volume.</p>
      <p>The fan-out compounds it. One Make.com scenario takes a single order webhook and branches in parallel to Meta CAPI, Google Enhanced Conversions, TikTok Events API, a Google Sheets inventory row and a Google Sheets P&amp;L row — five destinations, one trigger, roughly five operations. The same work in Zapier is five separate Zaps, each metering its own tasks, all requiring a paid tier for webhook support.</p>
      <p>Legitimate reasons to pick Zapier anyway: you need a connector in Zapier's larger library that Make.com lacks, your team's existing Zapier fluency outweighs the price gap, or you depend on Zapier Tables or Interfaces, which have no Make.com equivalent. For the standard Shopify jobs — tracking, inventory, email, P&amp;L — none of those apply.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- LIMITS & UPGRADE -->
  <section>
    <div class="inner-sm">
      <div class="tag">Capacity planning</div>
      <h2 id="limits-h">Free tier limits — and when to upgrade</h2>
      <p>The free plan is 1,000 credits a month with no time limit. What matters is how fast your stack consumes them, and that is a function of branches per order, not orders alone.</p>

      <div style="overflow-x:auto;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--border);margin-top:24px;">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background:var(--bg-3);">
              <th style="padding:12px 18px;text-align:left;color:var(--text-4);font-family:monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Orders/month</th>
              <th style="padding:12px 18px;text-align:left;color:var(--text-4);font-family:monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Credits used</th>
              <th style="padding:12px 18px;text-align:left;color:var(--text-4);font-family:monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Plan needed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[
              ["Under 200", "~1,000", "Free — $0/month"],
              ["200 – 2,000", "~10,000", "Core — $12/month"],
              ["2,000 – 10,000", "~60,000", "Pro — $16/month"],
            ].map((row, i) => (
              <tr style={`border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);background:${i%2===0?'transparent':'rgba(255,255,255,.015)'};`}>
                <td style="padding:11px 18px;color:var(--text-3);">{row[0]}</td>
                <td style="padding:11px 18px;color:var(--text-3);font-family:monospace;">{row[1]}</td>
                <td style="padding:11px 18px;color:var(--g);font-weight:600;">{row[2]}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:20px;">The arithmetic behind it: a four-branch scenario costs roughly five to six operations per order, so 1,000 credits carries about 160–200 orders a month on the full stack. Running only CAPI Shield — a much lighter scenario — stretches the same allowance considerably further. <strong>Count your branches before you count your orders.</strong></p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:32px;">The three upgrade triggers</h3>
      <p><strong>You are consistently hitting the credit ceiling before month end.</strong> This is the honest one. Scenarios stop running when credits are exhausted, and a tracking scenario that silently stops mid-month costs more in lost attribution than the $12 would have.</p>
      <p><strong>You need more than two active scenarios.</strong> The free plan caps active scenarios, which is why the recommended architecture is one scenario with a router rather than several small ones. If your logic genuinely will not consolidate, that is a real reason to move up.</p>
      <p><strong>You need scheduling tighter than the free interval.</strong> Webhook-triggered scenarios fire instantly on any plan, so this only bites for polling scenarios — reports, reconciliation, scheduled syncs — not for the order-driven automations on this page.</p>
      <p>Everything else is premature. Most Shopify stores under 200 orders a month run the complete stack on the free tier indefinitely, and the correct move at 250 orders is to consolidate branches into one router before paying for headroom you have not earned.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MISTAKES -->
  <section>
    <div class="inner-sm">
      <div class="tag">Failure modes</div>
      <h2 id="mistakes-h">Three mistakes that break a Shopify scenario quietly</h2>
      <p>None of these throw an error. Each one produces a scenario that runs green in Make.com while the data arriving at the other end is wrong.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:28px;">Not de-duplicating against the browser pixel</h3>
      <p>If you keep the Meta pixel on the storefront and add server-side events, both fire for the same purchase. Without a shared identifier, Meta counts two conversions and your reported ROAS inflates until you stop trusting it. Send the same <code>event_id</code> — the Shopify order ID works — on both the browser event and the server event, and the platform collapses them into one. This is the single most common way a first CAPI build makes attribution worse rather than better.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:28px;">Using the wrong TikTok event name</h3>
      <p>TikTok's purchase event is <code>CompletePayment</code>. Not <code>Purchase</code>, not <code>purchase</code>. The string is case-sensitive and TikTok accepts an unrecognised name without complaint — the events land in Events Manager under a category that no campaign is optimising against, so the scenario looks healthy and the ad account learns nothing.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:28px;">Ignoring webhook retries</h3>
      <p>Shopify retries a webhook when your endpoint does not acknowledge it quickly enough, which means the same order can arrive twice. For a tracking branch that is a duplicate conversion; for a Google Sheets inventory branch it is a duplicate row and a stock count that drifts. Key your Sheets writes on the order ID and check for an existing row before appending, rather than assuming each delivery is unique.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="alt-bg" id="faq">
    <div class="inner-sm">
      <div class="tag">FAQ</div>
      <h2 id="make-com-shopify-common-questions">Make.com + Shopify — Common Questions</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">Is Make.com free for Shopify automation?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">Make.com's free plan gives 1,000 credits/month (formerly called operations) with no time limit and no credit card required. It includes webhook triggers and multi-step scenarios — enough to run CAPI Shield, Stocky Swap, TikTok CAPI, and P&L Auto simultaneously for stores under ~250 orders per month.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">Does Make.com work with Shopify Basic?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">Yes. Make.com works with every Shopify plan including Basic. It connects via Shopify's built-in webhook system (Settings → Notifications → Webhooks) which is available on all plans. No Shopify app is required.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">What is the Make.com free plan limit for Shopify stores?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">1,000 credits/month (formerly called operations). Running all four Stack Architect scenarios simultaneously uses approximately 4–6 operations per order — covering roughly 166–250 orders per month free. Above that, Make.com Core at $12/month gives 10,000 credits.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">Can Make.com replace Zapier for Shopify?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">Yes — and it's significantly better value. Make.com provides 10× more free operations, includes webhook triggers on the free plan (Zapier requires a paid plan), and supports multi-step branching for free. For Shopify automation specifically, Make.com is the recommended platform at every price point.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">How long does it take to set up Make.com with Shopify?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">Connecting Make.com to Shopify via webhook takes approximately 3 minutes. Building each individual automation (CAPI Shield, Stocky Swap, TikTok CAPI, P&L Auto) takes 4–8 minutes each from the free guides. The Complete Kit ($29) provides pre-built JSON blueprints that import in 60 seconds each.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="faq-item">
          <p class="faq-q">What is a Make.com JSON blueprint for Shopify?</p>
          <p class="faq-a">A Make.com JSON blueprint (also called a scenario file) is a complete export of a finished automation — every module, API endpoint, and field mapping is embedded in the file. Importing it recreates the entire scenario in one click with zero manual configuration. The Complete Kit contains four pre-built blueprints for CAPI Shield, TikTok CAPI, Stocky Swap, and P&L Auto.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FINAL CTA -->
